.TH CALDUN 1 "2026-08-16" "caldun 1.7" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
caldun \- report trustworthy machine temperatures and alert when hot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B caldun
.RI [ --notify ]
.RI [ --popup ]
.RI [ --peak ]
.br
.B caldun --check
.br
.B caldun --json
.RI [ --notify ]
.br
.B caldun --get
.I CATEGORY
.br
.B caldun --watch
.RI [ N ]
.RI [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B caldun
auto-discovers the machine's reliable temperature sensors from
.BR sensors (1)
JSON output and reports them, flagging any that are running warm or critical.
Sensors are selected by a driver-class allowlist \(em CPU
.RB ( k10temp ", " coretemp ", " zenpower ", " acpitz ),
GPU
.RB ( amdgpu ", " radeon ", " i915 ", " nouveau )
and drives
.RB ( nvme ", " drivetemp ).
ISA Super-I/O chips (e.g.\&
.BR nct6798 )
are ignored by default because their channels are mislabeled on many boards and
report bogus values.
.PP
Thresholds are hybrid. When a sensor reports a hardware limit
.RI ( temp*_max " / " temp*_crit )
it is used; otherwise a per-category default applies. When both exist the lower
(more conservative) value wins, so a hardware limit can only make an alert fire
sooner. All thresholds and the sensor allow/ignore lists are configurable in
.IR /etc/caldun.conf .
.SS Suspected sensor faults
A real overheat heats every channel on a chip, so even the coolest is warm; a
buggy sensor leaves a sane channel reading cool. When an alarming channel sits
more than
.B ANOMALY_MARGIN
degrees above a sibling channel that is still below WARN, the reading is tagged
.BR SUSPECT :
it is listed separately and does
.I not
raise a thermal alarm or affect the exit status. Matching entries from
.IR /etc/caldun-known-issues.conf
add a defect description and a fix.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output modes
These are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.B --check
Self-test: list the discovered sensors and their effective WARN/CRITICAL
thresholds (and whether each came from hardware or a default), then exit.
.TP
.B --json
Emit the report as a single JSON object (schema 1) instead of formatted text,
for consumption by other programs. Colour is never emitted. The exit status is
unchanged, so a caller can use either. Fields per sensor include
.IR friendly ", " technical ", " chip ", " category ", " celsius ", " warn ", "
.IR crit ", " threshold_source ", " status " and " siblings_min ,
plus
.IR model ", " firmware ", " defect " and " solution
for suspect readings. The top level also carries
.IR cpu_clock " and " fans .
Consumers should check
.I schema
before relying on the layout.
.TP
.BI --get " CATEGORY"
Print one bare number \(em the temperature in degrees Celsius, with no unit,
status or decoration \(em and nothing else, so a script can compare or compute
with it directly.
.I CATEGORY
is
.BR cpu ", " gpu ", " drive " or " other .
When more than one sensor belongs to the category (two NVMe drives, say) the
.I hottest
wins, that being the reading a build script would gate on; append
.BI : CHIP
(for example
.BR drive:nvme-pci-0300 )
to name one exactly. Exits 4 if the category has no sensor on this machine, so
.B t=$(caldun --get gpu) || skip
works on a headless box.
.TP
.BR --watch " [" \fIN\fR ]
Sample every
.I N
seconds (default 30) until interrupted, printing one compact timestamped line
per sample:
.PP
.RS
.EX
2026\-08\-16T14:54:28 cpu=50 gpu=42 drive=64.9 cpu_mhz=2100 OK
.EE
.RE
.IP
Keys are category names, suffixed for duplicates
.RB ( drive ", " drive2 ).
Combined with
.BR --json ,
one JSON object per line (JSONL) is emitted instead. On exit a peak summary is
written to standard error. The configuration is read once at startup, not per
sample, so thresholds cannot change mid-log.
.PP
.RS
Watch mode is the one place where the exit status differs: it exits
.B 0
on a clean interrupt, because the status reports that the run ended, not how hot
the machine was on the last sample.
.RE
.SS Modifiers
.TP
.B --notify
Send a desktop notification via
.BR notify-send (1)
when any sensor is WARN or CRITICAL. In watch mode, notifications are rate
limited to one per sensor per state change.
.TP
.B --popup
Always send a desktop notification with the full reading, whatever the status.
.TP
.B --peak
Report the high-water mark recorded for each sensor since the machine booted.
Peaks are accumulated by every run, including the systemd timer's, so the
history is as fine-grained as the timer interval. Readings tagged
.B SUSPECT
are tracked separately and never become a sensor's peak, so a phantom spike
cannot poison the record.
.TP
.BR -V ", " --version
Print the program name and version, then exit. The version is also carried in
the
.B --json
output as the
.I version
field, alongside
.IR schema .
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH CPU CLOCK
When a percentage is shown next to the CPU clock it is measured against a
ceiling that
.B caldun
trusts. Under
.BR amd_pstate " / " intel_pstate ,
.I cpuinfo_max_freq
is the boost ceiling and is used directly. Under
.B acpi-cpufreq
it is only the
.I base
clock \(em on a Ryzen 7 4800U it reads 1.8 GHz while the part boosts to 4.2 GHz,
so the naive ratio exceeds 100% at idle \(em and no boost ceiling is exposed
anywhere in sysfs. There
.B caldun
uses the highest clock it has ever observed on the machine, and shows absolute
GHz only until it has enough samples for that to mean something. The reported
clock is the mean across cores, not the maximum: caldun is itself load on the
busiest core, so a maximum would read near boost on every sample.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All monitored sensors are within normal range.
.TP
.B 1
At least one sensor is at or above its WARN threshold.
.TP
.B 2
At least one sensor is at or above its CRITICAL threshold.
.TP
.B 3
A required command
.RB ( sensors " or " jq )
is not installed.
.TP
.B 4
No trustworthy sensors were found; run
.B sudo sensors-detect
or add sensors via
.B EXTRA_SENSORS
in the config file. Also returned by
.B --get
when the requested category has no sensor.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error: an unknown option, a missing option value, or two output modes at
once.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CALDUN_CONF
Path to the configuration file (default
.IR /etc/caldun.conf ).
.TP
.B CALDUN_ISSUES
Path to the known-issues database (default
.IR /etc/caldun-known-issues.conf ).
.TP
.B CALDUN_PEAKS
Path to the peak state file (default
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/caldun/peaks ).
.TP
.B CALDUN_SENSORS_JSON
Read sensor data from this file instead of running
.BR sensors (1).
Intended for testing on machines that have no trustworthy sensors of their own.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/caldun.conf
Configuration (thresholds, margins, sensor allow/ignore lists, clock and fan
display, peak tracking).
.TP
.I /etc/caldun-known-issues.conf
Known faulty-sensor database consulted for SUSPECT readings.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/caldun/peaks
Recorded high-water marks. Written by every run; reset when the machine reboots.
Safe to delete.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Gate a build on drive temperature:
.EX
[ "$(caldun --get drive)" \-lt 70 ] || echo "SSD too hot to start"
.EE
.TP
Log temperatures beside a long compile:
.EX
caldun --watch 10 | tee \-a build\-temps.log
.EE
.TP
Feed a monitoring system:
.EX
caldun --json | jq '.sensors[] | select(.status != "ok")'
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sensors (1),
.BR sensors-detect (8),
.BR notify-send (1)
